{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the dap mcp writer at head 141f31e3, for request 6db548f0 and review request d2764137. Verdict: APPROVED. The repair reads the record first, so a stop now says what happened under the key, and a kept answer needs no session. The head may land. The Worker bundle's bytes change, and hosted behaviour does not.\n\nYou corrected me on one point, and you are right. I wrote that `settle` still reaches a kept answer. It reads the target first, so past the window it fails there, and a repeat of the call reaches the answer. The note's table now states that.\n\n## Your four questions\n\n1. **`#membershipOf` returning `s.repository` after a failed room read: right.** It gives a failed read the same answer that a good read already gives: a scope with no membership of its own takes the room's. So the custody identity no longer depends on whether that read worked, and that is the root of the second intent you found. `#scope` is its only caller. Every read that takes this membership goes through `Reads.read`, which tries the session and falls back to a signed read on `forbidden`, so a person's reads lose nothing. `this.#port.originals` keeps a hosted agent on today's path. Finding this while the first repair stayed red was good work. I had called the same fallback a limit, and I did not see that it changed the identity.\n2. **The `stepped` sentence: right enough, with one limit.** Custody writes a step as `sent` before it delivers, so \"went\" is false only after a crash between that write and the send. \"Nothing was sent\" would be the larger error here. Leave it as it is. The note already says that a scripted record drives the test.\n3. **The two older false sentences: neither holds this head, and I would take 1df3a912 next.** Both predate this writer, and this head makes neither worse. But your note says that in a real outage a person meets `transport` more often than `session-lost`. So the case I blocked on stays open for the commonest outage until that request lands. \"Call again; nothing was submitted\" sits beside \"the outcome is unknown\". A model can read it as permission to use a new key. The planner orders the queue, so I state this as my recommendation to it.\n4. **A hosted agent's identity drift: acceptable to leave.** It predates this writer, and your repair's test shows what it does: a second intent under the same key gets `mismatch` from the scope, not a second entry. That is a safe refusal with poor words. Name it in 1df3a912 or a request of its own, so that it does not stay only in this note.\n\n## The repair\n\n- **The order.** `before` is read first. A terminal record that is not a resubmission takes the plain path, and `answered` ends any row. Its session ask cannot fail the call: `#endAnswered` and `#endOriginal` catch a lost reply, and no session means no end.\n- **The words.** `#sentBefore` feeds every stop, so `earlier` and `stepped` come from one place. `own` is now the plain opposite of `earlier`, which removes the branch I expected to go.\n- **The rows.** Removing `holder` removes the second path to a membership. The limit I asked for is in the note.\n- **The note's other changes.** The `expired` and agent sentences read as I asked, and the restart table has its rows. I read them against the code and did not run them.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n**Architecture.** Sound. One function now decides the custody identity's membership, and the writer has no separate rule for it.\n\n**Security.** No authority issue. A session reaches only its member's rows, and a row needs that key's signature. A hosted agent takes the plain path.\n\n**Simplification.** Better than the last head. `holder`, `again`, `sends` and two `own` conditions are gone.\n\n## What I checked\n\n- **Read.** The source diff from 0385a59e to 3be96040, the note's changes, `docs/mcp.md`, `Reads.read` and custody's step path. I did not read the new tests' bodies or the witness script.\n- **Evidence.** `SHA256SUMS` hashes to `ebcba3b5…`, each listed file verifies, and no file is writable. The gate's head is `fd165884`, it exited 0, and its log shows 3467 plain and 948 native tests. From 3be96040 to `fd165884`, the writer's sources and tests do not change. From there to this head, only the note and its manifest change. `controls/summary.jsonl` holds 41 rows that distinguish. `red/run.log` shows the three named cases failing by assertion at 0385a59e. The witness's failures are empty. I read those results and did not rerun them.\n- **Tests I ran at this head.** `mcp-original.scope.test.ts`, `hosted.scope.test.ts` and `hosted-loop.scope.test.ts` pass 34 of 34. `lost-reply.test.ts` and `errors.test.ts` pass 8 of 8. `security-entry-points.test.mjs` passes 10 with 0 failures. `check-kernel-boundary` reports 127 sources and 0 refusals. `check-notes` reports 0 warnings, `check-doc-links` 0 broken links, and `check-commit-messages` 0 flagged. The worktree stayed clean. The head includes main 1277da37.\n- **Not done.** I used no independent reader. No deployed Worker, real room, real `prepare` call or second physical device has run this writer.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0c2bf1249c9f3e516199301192c288f1f24ad424","binding":"assigned","head":"141f31e3dc7944dab0b08f9ee48954097d30c391","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2c321698611c366016e75e38f866223ca657c509\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:8c3989ce64531ec4b8cd5cc268af14e41296ee07\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2c321698611c366016e75e38f866223ca657c509","review_path":"reviewguard@1","stale":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0c2bf1249c9f3e516199301192c288f1f24ad424, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:1464f2d2cb6a022b23ec9827523e75b9cb096437, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f